MERGED-PR AUDIT #9046 — REOPENED. All seven `read-one-form` implementations wrap fixture text as `[
<text>
]`, call `clojure.edn/read-string`, and only require the returned vector to contain one element. The fixture can close that synthetic vector itself with `]`; `read-string` then returns the one-element vector and silently ignores everything after it, recreating the exact truncation class this bead was meant to remove.

Reproduced by invoking the merged `re-frame.conformance-test/read-one-form` directly with `{:fixture/id :first}
] {:fixture/id :silently-hidden}`. It returned `#:fixture{:id :first}` without throwing; the wrapped input visibly retained the hidden second map after the early `]`. The same eight-line body is present in all seven loader sites (six private copies plus the core owner).

The unconditional Python corpus scanner would reject this particular repository fixture, but that does not make the loaders' own "EXACTLY ONE top-level form" contract true: a targeted artefact suite can still accept the malformed fixture without running the scanner. Minimal repair: read the ORIGINAL text through a `PushbackReader`/`edn/read`, then perform a second read with an EOF sentinel (or otherwise prove EOF after the parsed form), and add a regression whose trailing text starts with `]` so the synthetic-envelope escape is discriminating. Keep the seven small copies if classpath reach still makes that the honest shape; no new shared production namespace is required.

